[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Last week we talked about a completely new production workflow that has the potential to fundamentally change the way we make films. It\'s called "gray boxing," and essentially you just need to shoot a version of your'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'film in a very simple environment with as even lighting as possible. And then, using artificial intelligence tools, you can take that simple scene and bring it into'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any environment. For example, we have this very simple piece with Joel, a member of the Curious Refuge community, hiding behind a car. After loading this video clip into C Dance 2.5 and'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'requesting a dystopian environment, this is what we got. And it\'s just incredible. In essence, we created an entire world that would be impossible and expensive'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to implement using traditional methods. This workflow is great, but there is one very obvious problem. Every time you hit " render," you don\'t actually have control over how the environment will look . You are just giving'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'him a hint. And because of this, there will be a lot of disagreements in your environment. What if you could have even more control over how your scene looks? Well , that\'s exactly what'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this video will be about. My name is Caleb. Welcome to Curious Refuge. Be sure to like and subscribe if you want to see more videos like this in the future. So, let\'s get to the point of this guide. For'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this test, I have a video of Joel running in our "gray box" environment. Again, it is lit by large diffusion screens to make'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the lighting as uniform as possible. You can see that our camera is following it from above, and we asked for a rocky terrain. The camera looks down to enhance'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sense of height at which it is located. You get the idea. Now we want to replace this studio environment with artificial intelligence. Normally, in such a workflow, you would have to take'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a video clip, upload it to SeaDance, enter a request for the necessary changes, and constantly redo everything, which can be quite expensive in SeaDance. So we\'ll do it differently: we\'ll take our first frame and modify it using'

[02:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'artificial intelligence tools for images. There are many ways to get a static image of that first frame, but in Adobe Premiere Pro, which I usually use for editing, you just need to import the footage.'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then there\'s a little camera icon to export the frame, and you can save it to your computer. Now, to style this opening frame, there are quite a few different tools you can use. Specifically, you have the GPT 2.5 SeaDream 5, as well as the Nano Banana'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pro or Nano Banana 2, although the Pro usually works better. I will be using GPT 2.5 because it is a newer model that just came out, but you can get decent'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'results with all of these tools. Sometimes it really depends on the overall scene you\'re working with, and we\'ll talk about that in a moment. So, to create your image, you will need to go to an'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI image aggregator. I use Magnific, but you can use Artlist, Korea, or any other aggregator you want. Now, as you can see, I have GPT 2.5 selected, but for the variant we will choose'

[03:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sunburst as it is a more intelligent version of the model. Now for our first loaded image, we have an initial frame in the “gray'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'box” environment. And for our second image, we have a version from Midjourney that reflects the color correction and overall cinematic style we\'re going for. For our prompt, we will specify: change the location and'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'surroundings in the first image to the edge of a cliff in Ireland. Use the second image as a reference for color correction, lighting, and style. Of course, if we want to add even more specificity'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about what we want to see in this scene, we can do that. We can add more supporting materials. We can detail the prompt. It really depends on the project you\'re working on. You have the ability'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to create multiple images at the same time. I recommend creating more than one and then you can change the resolution. You probably need to have at least 2K resolution to get the best quality. Now I'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set the setting to "high ". You can choose " maximum" if you want, but I recommend doing this only if you are not getting the desired result from the first generation. And when you\'re ready, feel free to click "'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Generate". After about a minute, we have a few options, but this one is my favorite. I think it looks very cinematic. I like color correction. I like that the lighting has changed , and I feel the tension'

[04:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from this rock that is so close by. And the overall angle is very accurate, it looks like we\'re still looking down over her shoulder. I think he did just fine. So we\'ll use this as our starting'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image. So now we have a reference image and a video of the actress running to the edge of an artificial cliff. The last thing I want to'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'create is a depth map. A depth map is essentially a black and white version of your scene that conveys volume. You can use it to help AI'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'better understand how objects in a scene should be arranged. This is also very useful when working with visual effects. This is an optional step. You don\'t have to use a'

[05:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depth map, sometimes the result is even better without it, but I want to compare the results. So to do this, I\'ll use the AI ​​video generator in Magnific, where we have SeaDance 2.5 selected. So, we have'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'both videos and the original reference image. For our query, we will specify: "Use image one". This is the initial frame, and then we\'ll use the video alone, in this case the'

[05:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depth map, as a reference for the movement. A woman runs to the edge of a cliff, there is no music, only ambient sound effects. This works sometimes, but you\'ll see everything in a moment. You have'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the ability to generate multiple frames at the same time. Depending on your budget, this may be an acceptable option . If not, you may have to do them one at a time. It takes more time, but you save money. So it'

[06:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all depends on the project you are working on. Regarding the resolution , you can choose up to 1080p and set the total duration to be equal to the duration of the input video. It\'s worth noting that this'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only works for clips up to 30 seconds long, which is usually enough for most movie scenes . Next, you select the 16:9 format and turn on the sound. You don\'t have to turn on'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sound, sometimes the model works better without it. So this is just an additional option. You may not choose it. And when you\'re ready, you can click " generate". I want to show you two'

