AUDIO: 'can see that she does reach out a little further than in the original video, but overall it\'s very similar to the original and the result is great. I was also curious what would happen'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if we took an edited image of Joel in a cardigan with a glass of wine in front of him, added that outfit and the reference video, and all of that together gave us this result. And yes, the result is'

[13:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really very good. The biggest problem, in my opinion, is that we shot in fairly even lighting, so the result looks a bit flat. So you should'

[13:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set up the lighting on site in advance, and then work with both the scene and the reference image to direct the lighting in the direction you need. Otherwise, it will likely look'

[13:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a bit flat. And we also wanted to test it using an image from Seed Dream, and this is what we got. Not bad. It\'s worth'

[13:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'noting that we tested this across a variety of genres, from science fiction to fantasy, and everywhere the result works quite well. If you want to do this yourself, I would advise planning'

[14:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'two things. First, you need to have a clear idea of ​​what your overall scene should look like in your simple surroundings. Although we shot in a gray studio for the test, you'

[14:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can of course use any regular space you have. What\'s more important is to understand the overall lighting you want to achieve. If your scene takes place in moonlight, it'

[14:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'may be worth setting up a large softbox above your subject\'s head to simulate that light. If it\'s light from a window, you might want to do the same thing, but place the light source on the side. It all depends on'

[14:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what overall look of the scene you are going for. If you don\'t know what the lighting should look like , you can of course shoot everything in even light and get pretty good results. But if you understand where the light sources should be during the shoot'

[14:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: ', I advise you to at least roughly take them into account when generating, as this will significantly improve the result. I hope you enjoyed this video, and of course, if you want to master the latest AI filmmaking workflows,'

